1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,000
Το Plex ζήτησε 120€ για να συνεχίσετε να βλέπετε από τις συσκευές σας.
Το πλήρωσα εγώ το ποσό.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:10,000
Αν χρησιμοποιείτε συχνά και μπορείτε,
 οποιαδήποτε συμβολή θα εκτιμηθεί.

3
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:22,600
It's been a while, Flamme.

4
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:57,060
<i>You're there like the wind</i>

5
00:00:57,060 --> 00:01:01,680
<i>Closing your eyes at sunset</i>

6
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:07,330
<i>I wonder what's on your mind</i>

7
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:16,030
<i>With your eyelids open</i>

8
00:01:16,030 --> 00:01:20,610
<i>Your eyes shone like glass</i>

9
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:27,380
<i>It faintly smelled like a sunny day</i>

10
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:38,510
<i>Clear up the sky, let flowers bloom</i>

11
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:42,710
<i>Bloom and blame it on the sunny sky</i>

12
00:01:42,710 --> 00:01:47,100
<i>Even the rain, when it stops</i>

13
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:52,100
<i>Will make you shine in the sunlight</i>

14
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:57,500
<i>Calm the stirring sounds in my heart</i>

15
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:01,540
<i>We go like the sunny wind</i>

16
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:05,950
<i>Go on and fly over that cloud</i>

17
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:10,780
<i>Further and further away</i>

18
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:22,980
Bargland.

19
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:46,520
The young are too hot-blooded.

20
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:50,150
The so-called privilege is nonsense.

21
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:53,900
What makes you say that?

22
00:02:54,410 --> 00:02:56,950
Do you even have to ask?

23
00:02:57,710 --> 00:03:03,050
The greatest joy of magic
lies in searching for it.

24
00:03:03,930 --> 00:03:05,570
That's all.

25
00:03:06,090 --> 00:03:07,060
I see.

26
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:12,730
Time passes quickly.

27
00:03:13,450 --> 00:03:17,810
The student I chose to train on a whim
has already returned with her own student.

28
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:19,690
This is Frieren.

29
00:03:20,790 --> 00:03:21,930
An elf, huh?

30
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:25,450
She's strong. I like her.

31
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:29,850
Tell me what spell you desire.

32
00:03:29,850 --> 00:03:31,650
I shall grant you just one.

33
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,590
A spell that I desire?

34
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:40,520
I have knowledge of nearly every grimoire
that's ever been written.

35
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:47,730
Mages spend their entire lives
searching for the spell they desire.

36
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:51,360
Tell me what yours is,
and I shall grant it to you.

37
00:03:52,330 --> 00:03:53,350
I don't want one.

38
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:59,720
The greatest joy of magic
lies in searching for it.

39
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:05,070
Flamme, this girl is no good after all.

40
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:09,140
She lacks a burning ambition.

41
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:14,600
Master, someday this girl
will defeat the Demon King.

42
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:20,560
It will be a mage like her
who clears the way to a peaceful era.

43
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:23,560
Are you saying I cannot?

44
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:27,550
As one who seeks battle,
you cannot kill the Demon King.

45
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:30,250
We can't do it.

46
00:04:31,790 --> 00:04:37,910
After all, you can't imagine yourself
living in a peaceful era, can you?

47
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:44,440
Frieren is a mage of a peaceful era.

48
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:52,240
Good answer.

49
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:55,490
This is how mages ought to be.

50
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:12,730
<i>Damn it. He won't freeze at all.</i>

51
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:15,210
<i>The difference between
our mana is too great.</i>

52
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:16,980
Nephtear.

53
00:05:25,010 --> 00:05:26,630
Weak.

54
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:31,290
Kanne, defense!

55
00:05:31,290 --> 00:05:32,160
I know!

56
00:05:35,470 --> 00:05:37,940
You two put on quite the show earlier,

57
00:05:38,330 --> 00:05:41,260
so I believe I know most of your tricks.

58
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:48,180
Relatively non-lethal ice magic,

59
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:51,210
and a mage who can't fight without water.

60
00:05:53,670 --> 00:05:55,930
What am I even doing here?

61
00:05:56,450 --> 00:05:58,930
This really is just babysitting.

62
00:06:05,370 --> 00:06:07,190
That's not a nice thing to say.

63
00:06:07,470 --> 00:06:12,610
Won't you run out of mana
casting such flashy spells?

64
00:06:13,490 --> 00:06:16,240
You graduated from magic school, didn't you?

65
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:22,690
Are the old folks still teaching their students
to fight defensive battles of attrition?

66
00:06:23,020 --> 00:06:24,340
What do you mean?

67
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:27,920
Let me lecture you on the history of magic.

68
00:06:29,330 --> 00:06:36,930
The defensive spell developed to combat
Zoltraak is highly resistant to magic itself,

69
00:06:36,930 --> 00:06:41,940
but its effectiveness against physical
objects has hardly changed since then.

70
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:45,340
Naturally, there's a clear reason for that.

71
00:06:46,490 --> 00:06:52,190
It's enough to defend against the physical
attacks of monsters and warriors.

72
00:06:52,810 --> 00:06:56,160
Defensive magic mustn't be over-engineered.

73
00:06:56,670 --> 00:06:59,830
If we aimed for perfection,
the technique would become too complex,

74
00:07:00,230 --> 00:07:03,210
which would have a fatal effect
on activation time.

75
00:07:03,810 --> 00:07:07,350
The history of magic is one
of attacking and defending.

76
00:07:07,350 --> 00:07:09,900
It's the reason there's a trend
for modern attack spells

77
00:07:09,900 --> 00:07:12,210
to involve controlling physical objects.

78
00:07:14,140 --> 00:07:16,180
Do you understand what I'm getting at?

79
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:20,570
Kanne, no! Move!

80
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:32,400
My spells overwhelm my opponents
with high-volume physical attacks.

81
00:07:33,830 --> 00:07:35,610
Now stay down.

82
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:41,160
Give me a break.

83
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:42,960
I'm not interested in hurting you.

84
00:07:43,550 --> 00:07:46,840
Or do you want me to kill you after all?

85
00:07:47,710 --> 00:07:50,830
Should you be approaching us so carelessly?

86
00:07:51,190 --> 00:07:54,590
Sixty percent of the human body
is made of water.

87
00:07:55,020 --> 00:07:56,590
So what?

88
00:07:57,390 --> 00:08:00,090
Magic is a world of visual imagination.

89
00:08:00,530 --> 00:08:04,620
Can you picture how that water is distributed
throughout a person's body,

90
00:08:05,110 --> 00:08:07,640
and how you'd remove and manipulate it?

91
00:08:08,050 --> 00:08:12,800
How would you do that to a mage
protected by a large amount of mana?

92
00:08:14,110 --> 00:08:17,360
Spells can't produce effects
you can't picture.

93
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:23,370
And even if there were water here,
a small girl like you is no threat to me.

94
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:28,080
Not even bluffing works?
I hate adults like you.

95
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:31,750
But I have figured out one thing.

96
00:08:32,050 --> 00:08:34,770
You're lying.

97
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:38,570
If you loved fighting enough
to lecture us on it,

98
00:08:38,570 --> 00:08:41,900
you would've beaten us
somewhere there was water.

99
00:08:42,510 --> 00:08:44,590
What are you so afraid of?

100
00:08:48,390 --> 00:08:50,160
Behave yourselves.

101
00:08:51,410 --> 00:08:54,650
My job is just to put you two to sleep.

102
00:08:55,250 --> 00:08:57,520
As long as Denken doesn't lose, that is.

103
00:09:10,930 --> 00:09:14,560
<i>At this rate, this battle will end
when she exhausts my mana.</i>

104
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:18,890
This is all a bit tough for this old body.

105
00:09:19,190 --> 00:09:23,370
My apologies, Frieren, but I'm
going to settle this quickly.

106
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:27,400
Waldgose.

107
00:09:29,420 --> 00:09:30,900
Daosdorg.

108
00:09:37,420 --> 00:09:41,150
What is that decrepit old man doing?

109
00:09:42,050 --> 00:09:45,160
That old man fights rather spectacularly.

110
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:47,370
Will the barrier hold?

111
00:09:47,370 --> 00:09:52,910
Of course. The barrier was created
by the great mage Serie.

112
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:56,370
It cannot be destroyed by brute force.

113
00:09:56,370 --> 00:09:58,960
Oh? That's good, then.

114
00:09:59,550 --> 00:10:02,050
By the way, since around yesterday evening,

115
00:10:02,050 --> 00:10:04,910
someone's been analyzing the barrier.

116
00:10:05,650 --> 00:10:07,150
Why?

117
00:10:07,150 --> 00:10:08,070
I don't know.

118
00:10:08,950 --> 00:10:11,510
In any case, they're wasting their time.

119
00:10:12,170 --> 00:10:15,810
Do you really think there's a mage
in this world greater than Serie?

120
00:10:24,700 --> 00:10:26,670
Catastravia.

121
00:10:35,410 --> 00:10:37,840
<i>Impossible. A counterattack?</i>

122
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:40,310
<i>That was a continuous volley
of spears of light.</i>

123
00:10:40,310 --> 00:10:41,920
<i>She shouldn't have had the opportunity.</i>

124
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:03,070
<i>I'm out of mana. So this is the end.</i>

125
00:11:03,470 --> 00:11:05,070
<i>I don't believe it.</i>

126
00:11:05,070 --> 00:11:09,820
<i>She only used basic combat
spells until the end.</i>

127
00:11:10,460 --> 00:11:15,910
<i>This was practically a combat
lesson for a trainee mage.</i>

128
00:11:16,740 --> 00:11:18,180
I'm surprised.

129
00:11:18,740 --> 00:11:22,580
Even at my level, there was
still someone above me.

130
00:11:22,580 --> 00:11:24,650
Tell me where Laufen is.

131
00:11:25,090 --> 00:11:26,820
I know you know.

132
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:29,150
I'm taking back our Stille.

133
00:11:30,130 --> 00:11:34,010
If you were in my position,
do you think you'd tell me?

134
00:11:34,880 --> 00:11:36,090
You're right.

135
00:11:46,390 --> 00:11:49,860
Laufen, you're hiding nearby, aren't you?

136
00:11:50,140 --> 00:11:52,380
Bring the Stille here.

137
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:56,470
If you don't, I'll kill Denken.

138
00:11:57,090 --> 00:12:00,870
Don't do it, Laufen.
She doesn't intend to kill me.

139
00:12:01,310 --> 00:12:02,590
You can tell, can't you?

140
00:12:02,590 --> 00:12:04,490
<i>The old man was beaten.</i>

141
00:12:04,490 --> 00:12:09,880
<i>She's a far better mage than me—
no, better than even Richter.</i>

142
00:12:10,450 --> 00:12:12,260
<i>But she underestimates us.</i>

143
00:12:12,260 --> 00:12:14,430
<i>She's not touching the old man's body.</i>

144
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:19,200
Don't use Jilwer. Stay hidden.

145
00:12:19,430 --> 00:12:22,840
If you release even the slightest bit
of mana, she'll detect you.

146
00:12:22,840 --> 00:12:26,410
<i>From this distance, I could
retrieve the old man and flee!</i>

147
00:12:26,410 --> 00:12:28,430
Don't. It's a trap.

148
00:12:28,430 --> 00:12:30,190
You leave me no choice.

149
00:12:30,190 --> 00:12:33,000
I don't really want to do this, but...

150
00:12:35,220 --> 00:12:36,450
Found you.

151
00:12:38,770 --> 00:12:40,330
You fool.

152
00:12:43,580 --> 00:12:45,480
You inexperienced fool.

153
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:48,980
You knew she wouldn't kill me.

154
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:54,020
But she planned to hurt you
without killing you.

155
00:12:54,360 --> 00:12:57,610
We still would've won.

156
00:12:58,970 --> 00:13:03,350
Why are you so desperate
to become a first-class mage?

157
00:13:03,350 --> 00:13:07,490
You've got wealth, influence,
and everything else you could want.

158
00:13:12,710 --> 00:13:16,700
My homeland is located deep
in the Northern Plateau.

159
00:13:17,270 --> 00:13:22,880
Because the demons are acting up, only
first-class mages can enter the area now.

160
00:13:23,730 --> 00:13:27,150
I wanted to visit a grave
I haven't been to in a while.

161
00:13:27,150 --> 00:13:28,670
That's all.

162
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:33,400
Shouldn't you go help the kids?

163
00:13:34,370 --> 00:13:37,780
Perhaps it's too late, though.

164
00:13:37,780 --> 00:13:42,480
Richter will kill them before he faces you.

165
00:13:42,940 --> 00:13:46,780
He's capable of making pragmatic choices.

166
00:13:46,780 --> 00:13:50,370
They'll be fine. Kanne and Lawine will win.

167
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:55,390
I just finished analyzing the barrier.

168
00:13:57,710 --> 00:14:00,300
I wasn't expecting Denken to lose.

169
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:02,380
Now, then.

170
00:14:03,040 --> 00:14:06,160
Babysitting time is over.

171
00:14:31,660 --> 00:14:34,330
He's actually trying to kill us now.

172
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:36,850
We won't last a minute against him.

173
00:14:37,980 --> 00:14:40,070
Analyzing the barrier?

174
00:14:40,450 --> 00:14:41,920
What do you mean?

175
00:14:45,010 --> 00:14:48,850
Frieren. Don't tell me, you...

176
00:14:58,150 --> 00:14:59,150
Genau.

177
00:14:59,960 --> 00:15:02,970
It's not a problem. It's impossible.

178
00:15:03,580 --> 00:15:05,790
Even if the world were turned upside down—

179
00:15:13,260 --> 00:15:14,250
It's going to break.

180
00:15:21,370 --> 00:15:26,000
Sense, who is that elf mage?

181
00:15:26,690 --> 00:15:30,510
According to the list of test-takers,
her name is Frieren.

182
00:15:30,830 --> 00:15:33,760
That's the name of the mage who
traveled with the party of heroes.

183
00:15:34,350 --> 00:15:37,820
She apparently had a holy emblem, too.

184
00:15:38,390 --> 00:15:41,190
So she's the last great mage.

185
00:15:41,970 --> 00:15:43,980
Now you've done it, Frieren.

186
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:48,420
It's been a thousand years,
and this is how you say hello?

187
00:15:49,650 --> 00:15:52,510
This is why I can't quit being a mage.

188
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:57,370
In the world of magic, sometimes
the world is turned upside down.

189
00:15:58,430 --> 00:16:01,910
I can't believe you actually
destroyed the barrier.

190
00:16:02,260 --> 00:16:04,300
Why would you do that?

191
00:16:04,300 --> 00:16:06,680
Because I felt sorry for Kanne.

192
00:16:07,110 --> 00:16:09,420
Magic should be free.

193
00:16:09,860 --> 00:16:12,940
She controls water, does she?

194
00:16:13,470 --> 00:16:15,630
Magic is a world of visual imagination.

195
00:16:16,290 --> 00:16:20,630
Can you imagine defeating a mage
who controls water in the rain?

196
00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:23,630
At the very least, I can't.

197
00:16:24,570 --> 00:16:27,020
I doubt Richter can either.

198
00:16:27,460 --> 00:16:28,780
The barrier...

199
00:16:29,670 --> 00:16:31,430
What happened?

200
00:16:31,430 --> 00:16:32,270
It's raining.

201
00:16:33,010 --> 00:16:35,590
No, I can figure out what happened later.

202
00:16:36,160 --> 00:16:37,520
I have to finish them now—

203
00:16:38,260 --> 00:16:40,420
You finally showed an opening.

204
00:16:40,850 --> 00:16:43,030
Your panic is disrupting your mana.

205
00:16:43,030 --> 00:16:45,120
This will only buy you time!

206
00:16:47,930 --> 00:16:53,800
I see now why you feared a spell
that could control water.

207
00:16:54,680 --> 00:17:00,550
This is the perfect example of the
high-volume physical attacks you mentioned.

208
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:04,300
Lawine, don't let him get away.

209
00:17:04,790 --> 00:17:06,440
Reamstroha.

210
00:17:09,350 --> 00:17:11,940
There's nowhere for me to run, anyway.

211
00:17:14,720 --> 00:17:17,110
This is why I don't like water.

212
00:17:31,310 --> 00:17:32,930
Looks like you won.

213
00:17:33,420 --> 00:17:35,220
Let's get away from here.

214
00:17:35,630 --> 00:17:37,720
The sun sets in two hours.

215
00:17:37,720 --> 00:17:39,720
You can conceal your mana
until then, can't you?

216
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:42,470
I don't have any mana left to conceal.

217
00:17:42,870 --> 00:17:45,050
I see. You did well.

218
00:17:46,070 --> 00:17:47,190
Don't pat my head.

219
00:17:50,940 --> 00:17:51,940
Sorry.

220
00:17:53,420 --> 00:17:55,150
It was inevitable.

221
00:18:00,360 --> 00:18:03,050
Denken and I are out of mana.

222
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:07,360
We have two hours left.

223
00:18:07,360 --> 00:18:09,110
We're finished.

224
00:18:12,320 --> 00:18:15,070
Laufen, can you dispel the binding?

225
00:18:15,070 --> 00:18:18,920
You must be joking.
This binding spell is ridiculously powerful.

226
00:18:19,720 --> 00:18:22,800
Then bring down the tree instead.

227
00:18:31,240 --> 00:18:33,410
Denken, there's nothing we can do.

228
00:18:34,010 --> 00:18:38,310
I don't have the mana left
to use Jilwer anymore.

229
00:18:39,060 --> 00:18:45,580
Richter and Laufen, what do you think
an imperial mage does in this situation?

230
00:18:46,640 --> 00:18:49,570
He keeps fighting until the end.

231
00:18:51,170 --> 00:18:55,580
We'll search for signs of battles fought
by other parties and track them down.

232
00:18:57,170 --> 00:18:58,830
You know, Fern,

233
00:18:59,270 --> 00:19:01,890
you're really rather boring.

234
00:19:03,920 --> 00:19:07,110
You seriously do only cast basic spells.

235
00:19:07,110 --> 00:19:09,780
You should direct your
complaints to Ms. Frieren.

236
00:19:10,950 --> 00:19:14,640
Come to think of it,
that elf is your master, right?

237
00:19:17,300 --> 00:19:20,550
It's her fault we're soaking wet right now.

238
00:19:20,550 --> 00:19:22,640
Make sure you give her an earful, all right?

239
00:19:23,410 --> 00:19:24,990
I intend to.

240
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,840
Also, she doesn't limit what spells
I cast outside of combat.

241
00:19:30,490 --> 00:19:34,140
Come to think of it, you did cast
a spell to catch that bird.

242
00:19:34,670 --> 00:19:36,840
What other spells can you use?

243
00:19:37,390 --> 00:19:38,930
Let's see...

244
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:41,950
I have a spell that lets you
see through clothes...

245
00:19:43,300 --> 00:19:44,670
I'm not going to cast it.

246
00:19:45,430 --> 00:19:49,340
I hate to interrupt your
pleasant conversation,

247
00:19:49,340 --> 00:19:52,490
but maybe you shouldn't reveal
too many of your tricks.

248
00:19:52,810 --> 00:19:56,770
From the second test onward,
we'll be competing against one another.

249
00:19:57,480 --> 00:20:00,110
You're right.

250
00:20:00,310 --> 00:20:01,620
Sorry 'bout that.

251
00:20:02,430 --> 00:20:06,610
But I think you should've opened up
some more, four-eyes.

252
00:20:11,400 --> 00:20:12,790
There they are.

253
00:20:12,790 --> 00:20:14,700
It's a defeated party.

254
00:20:15,240 --> 00:20:18,820
The corpse of one of their
members was nearby.

255
00:20:18,820 --> 00:20:24,550
Even though there's no way for them to pass,
they're keeping a tight hold on their Stille.

256
00:20:28,570 --> 00:20:31,260
Are we going to negotiate with them, Denken?

257
00:20:31,260 --> 00:20:32,980
They wouldn't cooperate.

258
00:20:32,980 --> 00:20:37,690
If I were in their position,
I'd refuse even money.

259
00:20:38,120 --> 00:20:39,100
Why?

260
00:20:39,520 --> 00:20:42,060
It wouldn't sit right with me.

261
00:20:42,060 --> 00:20:44,240
It's not about logic.

262
00:20:45,680 --> 00:20:47,980
Laufen, wait here.

263
00:20:47,980 --> 00:20:50,210
If you see an opportunity,
take their Stille.

264
00:20:50,210 --> 00:20:52,160
Richter, come with me.

265
00:20:53,250 --> 00:20:54,680
What do you intend to do?

266
00:21:05,580 --> 00:21:07,900
We're approaching them head-on?

267
00:21:07,900 --> 00:21:10,260
We don't have any mana left.

268
00:21:11,580 --> 00:21:13,470
Don't you get it?

269
00:21:14,830 --> 00:21:17,970
They're in the same boat.

270
00:21:26,030 --> 00:21:27,770
Prepare yourself.

271
00:21:31,970 --> 00:21:33,910
You're a man, aren't you?

272
00:21:35,340 --> 00:21:36,990
You're joking, right?

273
00:21:39,730 --> 00:21:43,890
Let's settle this with our fists!

274
00:22:10,410 --> 00:22:11,740
It's time.

275
00:22:13,480 --> 00:22:17,420
The first test is now over.

276
00:22:18,150 --> 00:22:22,290
Six parties—eighteen individuals—
have passed the first test.

277
00:22:22,720 --> 00:22:24,790
The second test will take place
three days from now.

278
00:22:25,150 --> 00:22:27,870
You will be informed of the details later.

279
00:22:28,860 --> 00:22:31,070
That's all. You are dismissed.

280
00:22:34,060 --> 00:22:37,550
It's been two days since
they left for the exam.

281
00:22:38,210 --> 00:22:42,550
I can stay up as late as I want,
and Fern isn't around to get mad at me.

282
00:22:42,550 --> 00:22:44,900
I'm so happy, it's scary.

283
00:22:46,490 --> 00:22:48,830
Sounds like you've got it rough, buddy.

284
00:22:50,060 --> 00:22:53,200
<i>And I'm alright</i>

285
00:22:53,200 --> 00:22:56,120
<i>Yeah, I hear you</i>

286
00:22:56,120 --> 00:23:01,700
<i>The wind teases this hair that's longer now</i>

287
00:23:01,700 --> 00:23:04,140
<i>There's meaning in everything</i>

288
00:23:04,640 --> 00:23:07,120
<i>Even the days that came to a halt</i>

289
00:23:07,720 --> 00:23:13,760
<i>Now that I finally understand,
I've caught up to you</i>

290
00:23:13,760 --> 00:23:17,550
<i>See, things that couldn't have been</i>

291
00:23:17,550 --> 00:23:20,450
<i>Seen if it weren't for these eyes</i>

292
00:23:20,450 --> 00:23:25,120
<i>Why? They begin to overflow</i>

293
00:23:25,120 --> 00:23:31,350
<i>I won't ask to see you, so at least</i>

294
00:23:31,350 --> 00:23:37,140
<i>Let me remember the past, just for today</i>

295
00:23:37,140 --> 00:23:43,150
<i>So, if we were to meet again</i>

296
00:23:43,150 --> 00:23:49,030
<i>No more hesitations, I'd choose the present</i>

297
00:23:50,090 --> 00:23:53,050
<i>Anytime, anywhere, yah</i>

298
00:23:53,050 --> 00:23:57,700
<i>Anytime, anywhere</i>

299
00:23:57,700 --> 00:24:00,680
<i>I'll be there (close your eyes,
and I'll always be there)</i>

300
00:24:00,680 --> 00:24:03,430
<i>Even if there are no absolutes</i>

301
00:24:03,430 --> 00:24:06,500
<i>I'm always aware of your thoughts</i>

302
00:24:06,500 --> 00:24:12,530
<i>Those tears will be alright,
dawn will surely break</i>

303
00:24:12,980 --> 00:24:19,440
<i>I'm whispering our lullaby
for you to come back home</i>

304
00:24:20,100 --> 00:24:21,240
I'm not sharing.

305
00:24:20,680 --> 00:24:23,000
Don't worry, Ms. Frieren.

306
00:24:21,040 --> 00:24:23,000
Don't talk about that.

307
00:24:22,670 --> 00:24:26,040
You're even more spoiled than me.

308
00:24:23,020 --> 00:24:26,060
It's not my birthday.
I can't indulge myself like this.

309
00:24:23,800 --> 00:24:26,060
You must eat your vegetables, too.

310
00:24:26,060 --> 00:24:27,960
This guy is a pain in the butt.

311
00:24:26,280 --> 00:24:27,970
We're no longer party members.

312
00:24:27,970 --> 00:24:30,110
Next time, we'll be enemies.
